WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.576 --> 00:00:12.483
It was the navy's Fleet Week.

2
00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:16.166
The bay was full of ships for people to see and visit.

3
00:00:17.107 --> 00:00:21.110
Orson stood with his cousin Nick on top of a long black submarine.

4
00:00:22.451 --> 00:00:25.553
Nick was a sailor who worked aboard this underwater vessel.

5
00:00:25.573 --> 00:00:28.295
"What does this submarine do?"

6
00:00:28.675 --> 00:00:29.356
Orson asked.

7
00:00:30.996 --> 00:00:34.498
"The navy uses submarines to help protect our country," Nick said.

8
00:00:34.518 --> 00:00:37.700
"Most of our missions are for military research.

9
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.041
Ready to see inside?"

10
00:00:40.481 --> 00:00:41.402
Orson shivered.

11
00:00:42.202 --> 00:00:43.663
The sub had no windows.

12
00:00:44.423 --> 00:00:45.464
It seemed scary.

13
00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:51.427
Nick climbed down the ladder inside the hatch, the round opening that led into the sub.

14
00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:54.589
"Come on down," he called.

15
00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.732
Orson cautiously stepped onto the ladder's first rung.

16
00:01:01.044 --> 00:01:02.925
"Will the sub sink while we're on board?"

17
00:01:02.945 --> 00:01:09.227
"Subs control when they submerge," Nick assured him as they climbed down the ladder.

18
00:01:09.247 --> 00:01:12.169
"Tanks filled with air help them float.

19
00:01:14.349 --> 00:01:16.530
To submerge, we pump water into the tanks.

20
00:01:17.791 --> 00:01:19.832
That makes the sub heavier, so it goes down."

21
00:01:19.852 --> 00:01:22.933
"How does it get back up?"

22
00:01:25.014 --> 00:01:27.015
"We fill the tanks with air again to make it float."

23
00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:30.336
Nick grinned at him.

24
00:01:31.028 --> 00:01:32.229
"We won't submerge today."

25
00:01:33.509 --> 00:01:35.290
Orson reached the bottom of the ladder.

26
00:01:36.871 --> 00:01:38.591
"Why is it so cramped in here?

27
00:01:39.292 --> 00:01:41.313
The sub looks huge on the outside."

28
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:43.994
"Nuclear subs are big.

29
00:01:45.214 --> 00:01:49.676
This one is over ninety meters long, but the power plant and equipment take up most of the

30
00:01:49.716 --> 00:01:52.597
space," said Nick.

31
00:01:52.697 --> 00:01:54.938
"The 135 crew members get any room that's left."

32
00:01:56.279 --> 00:01:56.799
He laughed.

33
00:01:56.819 --> 00:02:01.813
"Some subs, like the ones they use for scientific research, are powered with oil.

34
00:02:03.293 --> 00:02:05.474
Oil-powered subs must surface every few days.

35
00:02:07.454 --> 00:02:09.555
Nuclear subs can stay underwater for months."

36
00:02:09.575 --> 00:02:16.177
"Don't you run out of water and air when the sub stays underwater that long?"

37
00:02:17.257 --> 00:02:18.958
"We make our own fresh water and air."

38
00:02:20.778 --> 00:02:22.259
"We made fresh water in school!"

39
00:02:22.619 --> 00:02:23.279
Orson said.

40
00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:26.840
"We boiled salt water and saved the condensed steam.

41
00:02:28.052 --> 00:02:28.833
Is that what you do?"

42
00:02:28.853 --> 00:02:31.875
"Exactly," said Nick.

43
00:02:33.376 --> 00:02:37.379
"We make enough water for crew members to drink and take a three-minute shower every day.

44
00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.762
Most of the water, though, is used to cool equipment."

45
00:02:42.143 --> 00:02:43.263
"How do you make air?"

46
00:02:43.283 --> 00:02:46.085
"We take oxygen from water.

47
00:02:47.787 --> 00:02:51.890
And we can reuse existing air by getting rid of the carbon dioxide people breathe out."

48
00:02:53.091 --> 00:02:54.612
"Cool," Orson said.

49
00:02:55.952 --> 00:02:58.794
Nick led Orson through rooms filled with computers.

50
00:03:00.195 --> 00:03:04.017
"We navigate with GPS on the surface, but GPS doesn't work underwater.

51
00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:08.219
So we navigate by keeping track of our direction and speed."

52
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:12.261
"How do you keep the sub from crashing into things?"

53
00:03:12.281 --> 00:03:15.143
"We use sound.

54
00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:17.144
We listen for sounds from other ships.

55
00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:20.406
We also use a device called sonar.

56
00:03:21.446 --> 00:03:24.248
It bounces sound off objects to detect and locate them."

57
00:03:25.548 --> 00:03:26.669
"That's what dolphins do!"

58
00:03:26.969 --> 00:03:27.650
Orson said.

59
00:03:29.491 --> 00:03:31.373
They climbed down a narrow stairway.

60
00:03:32.534 --> 00:03:35.697
Here beds were stacked three-high along the walls.

61
00:03:35.717 --> 00:03:38.299
"This is the crew's quarters."

62
00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.082
Nick pointed to a small drawer under one bed.

63
00:03:42.102 --> 00:03:44.244
"I keep all my stuff here."

64
00:03:44.264 --> 00:03:50.950
"Everything?" Orson had a room full of stuff at home.

65
00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.412
"What do you do all day?"

66
00:03:54.976 --> 00:03:56.197
"I'm on watch for eight hours.

67
00:03:57.318 --> 00:03:58.939
That's when I'm helping to operate the sub.

68
00:04:00.221 --> 00:04:03.703
When I'm not on watch, I still have to work to help clean and maintain the sub.

69
00:04:04.484 --> 00:04:05.725
We get some free time too.

70
00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:08.327
Then I sleep for eight hours."

71
00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:11.930
Suddenly the sub rocked.

72
00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:15.633
"Whoa!" Orson said.

73
00:04:15.653 --> 00:04:18.296
"Don't worry," Nick said.

74
00:04:18.316 --> 00:04:23.480
"Waves rock subs on the surface, but it's smooth sailing underwater."

75
00:04:25.424 --> 00:04:26.085
"I believe you."

76
00:04:26.445 --> 00:04:27.366
Orson smiled.

77
00:04:27.386 --> 00:04:31.349
"Living underwater seems like living in a spaceship!

78
00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:34.552
But I think I like living on land better!"
